WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.196 --> 00:00:02.087
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:02.087 --> 00:00:03.222
Welcome to another YouTube hangout.

00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:06.682
Super excited to be here
with an old friend, Amy,

00:00:06.682 --> 00:00:10.349
from Savvy, Sexy, Social,
and a new website,

00:00:11.278 --> 00:00:12.791
Vlog Like a Boss, to celebrate the launch

00:00:12.791 --> 00:00:15.594
of her new book that just came out

00:00:15.594 --> 00:00:18.466
on how you can have success
as a vlogger on YouTube.

00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:20.659
Amy, great to have you back on.

00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:22.236
- Evan, I'm so happy to be back.

00:00:22.236 --> 00:00:23.094
Thank you for having me.

00:00:23.094 --> 00:00:25.504
I love that I can join you in
this whole book club thing.

00:00:25.504 --> 00:00:26.600
Isn't that cool?

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.326
- Yeah, I didn't know that
our books were going to be

00:00:28.326 --> 00:00:29.771
so closely, a.

00:00:29.771 --> 00:00:30.604
- I know.

00:00:30.604 --> 00:00:31.437
It was so weird.

00:00:31.437 --> 00:00:32.270
- It's fun.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.943
Anyway, so today, we're talkin' about tips

00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:34.932
and tricks for YouTube success.

00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:36.322
I want to touch on your book

00:00:36.322 --> 00:00:38.425
and everything that you're
highlighted in there,

00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:40.756
but kind of the high-level first tip

00:00:40.756 --> 00:00:41.726
that you want to give out.

00:00:41.726 --> 00:00:44.857
What do you think holds
people back on YouTube

00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:47.281
from having the success
that maybe they deserve

00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:48.232
or should have?

00:00:48.232 --> 00:00:49.079
What do you think?

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:50.617
- I think it can be a little overwhelming

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.002
for people to consider talking on camera.

00:00:53.002 --> 00:00:55.822
I mean, even I, my
sister's a business owner,

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:57.551
and she's still trying to wrap her mind

00:00:57.551 --> 00:00:58.558
around the whole thing, you know.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.376
It's not just like listening
to me doesn't always mean

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:02.370
it's going to happen,
but a lot of people look

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.117
at the camera, and they're like, oh,

00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:05.396
that's a little intimidating.

00:01:05.396 --> 00:01:07.979
I think that holds a lot of people back.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:09.372
That's sort of like the first thing

00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:11.900
cause we're in our own
heads a little bit too much.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.066
And also, YouTube is kind
of a monster to take on.

00:01:16.066 --> 00:01:17.661
It's not the same as all
the other social networks

00:01:17.661 --> 00:01:19.837
because if you're going
into YouTube fresh,

00:01:19.837 --> 00:01:22.062
it's fresh, and there's
not a whole lot happening.

00:01:22.062 --> 00:01:24.537
So that can take some time to
kind of grow momentum there.

00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:27.440
So it's all of those things considered

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.511
that can make somebody kind of shut down

00:01:29.511 --> 00:01:31.255
and not get started at all.

00:01:31.255 --> 00:01:34.531
But I think the biggest
issue with that barrier

00:01:34.531 --> 00:01:39.079
is not really zeroing in on
the importance of your message.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:41.075
Because I think once you do that,

00:01:41.075 --> 00:01:44.182
a lot of fears and
walls start to fall down

00:01:44.182 --> 00:01:46.089
because if something is so
important that you've got

00:01:46.089 --> 00:01:47.485
to get it out there, and you know someone

00:01:47.485 --> 00:01:49.616
that needs to hear it, and
you have this information

00:01:49.616 --> 00:01:51.884
inside of you or this
entertainment inside of you

00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:54.174
that you just must share,

00:01:54.174 --> 00:01:57.416
when you focus on that and that
becomes the important thing,

00:01:57.416 --> 00:02:01.166
we can start to not be
so afraid of the things

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:03.572
that are holding us back internally.

00:02:03.572 --> 00:02:05.040
It's the importance of the audience

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.820
and what you have to deliver for them.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.889
- So just as you're saying that,

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.049
we already have people
joining live watching.

00:02:11.049 --> 00:02:11.882
- [Amy] Cool.

00:02:11.882 --> 00:02:12.715
- And if you guys watching live,

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:14.334
if you have a question for Amy,

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.872
put it in all caps, so it's easy to find.

00:02:16.872 --> 00:02:21.269
Clashgamerxp is asking,
talking on camera looks scary,

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:24.251
and he's concerned about
the idea that the video's

00:02:24.251 --> 00:02:26.135
going to be there forever.

00:02:26.135 --> 00:02:26.968
- [Amy] Right.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:27.816
- So this first video that you have

00:02:27.816 --> 00:02:28.729
that may not be good quality.

00:02:28.729 --> 00:02:31.959
It's your first attempt, that
it's going to live forever.

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:33.791
And people will always be able to see it.

00:02:33.791 --> 00:02:35.912
What advice do you have there?

00:02:35.912 --> 00:02:38.189
- So everyone always asks
this question, right.

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:40.169
Like, oh, I'm so much
better than I was before.

00:02:40.169 --> 00:02:41.748
Maybe I can delete my old stuff,

00:02:41.748 --> 00:02:44.527
and I actually think that that's
something you shouldn't do,

00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:47.321
and you should be totally
fine with publishing it.

00:02:47.321 --> 00:02:51.357
The luxury we have on YouTube
is building the archive.

00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:53.957
We want that video to be there forever.

00:02:53.957 --> 00:02:55.958
It's not very many places
where we can create

00:02:55.958 --> 00:02:57.349
something that's valuable for someone

00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:00.993
and it still be working for
us for five, six years later.

00:03:00.993 --> 00:03:03.975
One of my most popular videos
is from a very long time ago.

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:05.678
You know, maybe my hair wasn't perfect.

00:03:05.678 --> 00:03:06.898
Maybe the environment wasn't perfect.

00:03:06.898 --> 00:03:08.138
Lighting wasn't that good,

00:03:08.138 --> 00:03:10.087
but that video is still crushing it,

00:03:10.087 --> 00:03:11.355
and it's helping people.

00:03:11.355 --> 00:03:13.353
So I would challenge you to think how,

00:03:13.353 --> 00:03:16.221
if I'm going to be embarrassed
of this video down the road,

00:03:16.221 --> 00:03:18.823
have it be for the wrong reasons

00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:21.655
so that the right reasons are so strong,

00:03:21.655 --> 00:03:23.967
like how well you
delivered the information

00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:26.070
and how confident you
were and how evergreen

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.090
that is going to be to help
you over the long-term.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.934
That it's going to stay
there and work for you,

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:32.264
and you won't want to delete it.

00:03:32.264 --> 00:03:35.016
I actually think it's just
so important to hit publish,

00:03:35.016 --> 00:03:37.224
and practice, practice,
practice, practice.

00:03:37.224 --> 00:03:38.526
The only way to practice
and we're preaching

00:03:38.526 --> 00:03:41.193
to the choir here cause Evan
practices the publish button

00:03:41.193 --> 00:03:43.417
every single day very regularly,

00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:44.250
many times a day.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.518
You got to do that and
get the content out there

00:03:46.518 --> 00:03:49.102
to get the feedback,
to get better, to grow.

00:03:49.102 --> 00:03:50.930
And if I didn't have those first videos

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.655
that are like (hums) just okay,

00:03:53.655 --> 00:03:55.182
I wouldn't be here right now.

00:03:55.182 --> 00:03:56.706
And that's what keeps people, I think,

00:03:56.706 --> 00:04:00.085
from getting better is because
they get too much, again,

00:04:00.085 --> 00:04:01.838
in their own heads about
it and they're like,

00:04:01.838 --> 00:04:03.830
ah, well, I don't really
like how it looks,

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.280
so I'm just going to stop.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.494
Well, it's not going to get better then.

00:04:06.494 --> 00:04:09.772
You've got to practice, so
I would say, get into it.

00:04:09.772 --> 00:04:11.578
Be okay with the fact, I say this

00:04:11.578 --> 00:04:12.832
from the stage all the time.

00:04:12.832 --> 00:04:14.874
When I'm speaking about video, I say,

00:04:14.874 --> 00:04:17.562
you're first video is going to suck.

00:04:17.562 --> 00:04:18.604
Like it's going to be bad.

00:04:18.604 --> 00:04:19.437
- [Evan] Right.

00:04:19.437 --> 00:04:20.270
- Let's just put it out there.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:21.703
It's going to be bad, so
you better just get it done,

00:04:21.703 --> 00:04:23.054
and then, move on to the next one

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:24.414
and the next one, and
they're going to suck

00:04:24.414 --> 00:04:28.082
much more progressively
less than the previous.

00:04:28.082 --> 00:04:30.205
And you'll be in a good place before long.

00:04:30.205 --> 00:04:31.889
- I think, too, it shows you the journey.

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:34.159
- Yeah, I love that.
- A journey of inspiration.

00:04:34.159 --> 00:04:34.992
Right?

00:04:34.992 --> 00:04:36.142
If people look at you know, and say,

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:37.313
Amy, you're such a natural.

00:04:37.313 --> 00:04:38.146
You're so great at this.

00:04:38.146 --> 00:04:40.102
And meanwhile, you've
done all these videos.

00:04:40.102 --> 00:04:41.677
Like, go back. Look at my first one.

00:04:41.677 --> 00:04:42.899
It wasn't so good.

00:04:42.899 --> 00:04:43.732
- [Amy] Yeah.

00:04:43.732 --> 00:04:44.565
- And they can see that journey

00:04:44.565 --> 00:04:46.359
as an inspiration for the
work that you're doing.

00:04:46.359 --> 00:04:48.555
- Absolutely, it sort
of meta though for me,

00:04:48.555 --> 00:04:50.858
cause I get the luxury of being like,

00:04:50.858 --> 00:04:53.525
okay, I could take it as a total

00:04:54.378 --> 00:04:56.907
time to be like sad
inside when someone says,

00:04:56.907 --> 00:05:00.557
oh, my gosh, Amy, I was
so afraid to make a video.

00:05:00.557 --> 00:05:02.364
But then I watched your first videos,

00:05:02.364 --> 00:05:05.353
and I'm thinking, I'm going
to go ahead and do it, too.

00:05:05.353 --> 00:05:07.895
Because if you can do it, and
those were your first videos,

00:05:07.895 --> 00:05:08.728
then I can do it, too.

00:05:08.728 --> 00:05:09.677
That could sound really bad like,

00:05:09.677 --> 00:05:11.881
I would take that personal, but I don't.

00:05:11.881 --> 00:05:13.612
Because I sort of have this meta situation

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:16.184
of my job is to make you want to do video,

00:05:16.184 --> 00:05:18.649
so my journey makes you want to do video.

00:05:18.649 --> 00:05:20.106
Cause you can see how far I've come,

00:05:20.106 --> 00:05:21.469
and therefore, you can, too.

00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:22.909
But I think you can feel that way even

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:25.247
if you're not trying to tell people how

00:05:25.247 --> 00:05:27.315
to share their story with video.

00:05:27.315 --> 00:05:29.574
You can still feel that way
about your own database.

00:05:29.574 --> 00:05:32.554
It's got to be there, and we
see it all the time on YouTube

00:05:32.554 --> 00:05:35.186
where people feel like their
YouTube channel looks so bare.

00:05:35.186 --> 00:05:36.641
Well, that's because you're
afraid to hit publish

00:05:36.641 --> 00:05:38.708
on everything, so just get it out there,

00:05:38.708 --> 00:05:39.953
and do your best work.

00:05:39.953 --> 00:05:41.162
- Do you have a recommendation for

00:05:41.162 --> 00:05:43.821
how much people should be practicing.

00:05:43.821 --> 00:05:46.751
- Well, here's the crazy
thing is that going

00:05:46.751 --> 00:05:50.918
to YouTube Next Stop, they
really brought me back in terms

00:05:52.689 --> 00:05:54.221
of like how often you
should be publishing.

00:05:54.221 --> 00:05:56.174
Cause I was publishing super regularly.

00:05:56.174 --> 00:05:57.594
I was one of the only people there

00:05:57.594 --> 00:05:59.328
that had a regular schedule.

00:05:59.328 --> 00:06:01.672
Everyone else was like,
whenever I can get it done.

00:06:01.672 --> 00:06:03.375
I was publishing three
days a week religiously

00:06:03.375 --> 00:06:04.878
for a number of years.

00:06:04.878 --> 00:06:07.218
But industry standard on
YouTube is once a week.

00:06:07.218 --> 00:06:09.297
And I found that interesting
because I do think

00:06:09.297 --> 00:06:12.085
that's a good recommendation
if you're just getting started.

00:06:12.085 --> 00:06:14.758
Do a really good job on
your once a week video,

00:06:14.758 --> 00:06:16.745
and then learn how to market it.

00:06:16.745 --> 00:06:18.853
Because when you do that,
and you don't just get

00:06:18.853 --> 00:06:21.054
into the churn of doing
videos all the time,

00:06:21.054 --> 00:06:22.689
you learn how to market really well.

00:06:22.689 --> 00:06:25.185
And then, every video
works its hardest for you

00:06:25.185 --> 00:06:26.377
because at the beginning, especially,

00:06:26.377 --> 00:06:28.539
you've got to get some momentum there

00:06:28.539 --> 00:06:30.118
to start growing subscribers and views.

00:06:30.118 --> 00:06:32.283
So it is better to make
something really good,

00:06:32.283 --> 00:06:33.824
and then spend time marketing it,

00:06:33.824 --> 00:06:37.146
so you can make every project
after it matter even more.

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.822
But I can also see people
posting more than that

00:06:38.822 --> 00:06:41.644
because it's 2017, and
we've got smart phones.

00:06:41.644 --> 00:06:43.308
And there's a million
different ways to make video.

00:06:43.308 --> 00:06:45.595
And soon, I think we're
going to be more seamlessly

00:06:45.595 --> 00:06:47.724
live-streaming to YouTube with our phones.

00:06:47.724 --> 00:06:50.774
So there's a lot of ways you
can publish more regularly,

00:06:50.774 --> 00:06:52.931
but I think we should
be thinking about that

00:06:52.931 --> 00:06:55.353
and adding in the
component of how much time

00:06:55.353 --> 00:06:58.175
does it take to make
the video and market it.

00:06:58.175 --> 00:07:00.310
So that we're making the
most of all of that time.

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.911
Cause on YouTube, you're going
to need it if you want to grow.

00:07:03.911 --> 00:07:05.859
- So, I mean, I'm sure you get into this

00:07:05.859 --> 00:07:07.274
in your Vlog Like a Boss book.

00:07:07.274 --> 00:07:08.475
You guys can go check that out,

00:07:08.475 --> 00:07:10.191
but in terms of the marketing side.

00:07:10.191 --> 00:07:13.294
So if I've now made a new vlog, you know,

00:07:13.294 --> 00:07:16.849
Evan visits New York
city, and here's my day.

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:20.008
What am I now doing to market
it for the rest of the week?

00:07:20.008 --> 00:07:22.466
- Well, I think what's
really cool is first of all,

00:07:22.466 --> 00:07:24.848
really staying focused on a theme of like,

00:07:24.848 --> 00:07:26.085
what is this video about?

00:07:26.085 --> 00:07:28.088
It's not just every
single second of the day.

00:07:28.088 --> 00:07:29.074
Let's share it.

00:07:29.074 --> 00:07:30.408
But what is this video really about?

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:32.974
What is the take-away
we want people to have?

00:07:32.974 --> 00:07:34.180
Because I think that's going to help you

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.500
have more soundbites within.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.684
You can market a video
as a whole, you know,

00:07:38.684 --> 00:07:40.724
across your social media,
but there might also be

00:07:40.724 --> 00:07:43.899
bits and pieces inside that
video that you can share.

00:07:43.899 --> 00:07:45.508
Maybe there's a good Instagram moment.

00:07:45.508 --> 00:07:46.926
Maybe there's a behind the scenes thing

00:07:46.926 --> 00:07:48.436
you can be doing with Snapchat.

00:07:48.436 --> 00:07:50.360
I don't think people think
about the repurposing

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.056
of a video quite enough
because we put so much energy

00:07:53.056 --> 00:07:55.079
into creating one project.

00:07:55.079 --> 00:07:57.301
But there's also probably
10 different things in there

00:07:57.301 --> 00:07:59.959
that would make that one
piece of content marketable.

00:07:59.959 --> 00:08:03.266
And so how can you livestream
something on Instagram

00:08:03.266 --> 00:08:05.174
while you're making that project

00:08:05.174 --> 00:08:06.947
to get the anticipation up about it?

00:08:06.947 --> 00:08:09.074
Is there a screen capture from that video

00:08:09.074 --> 00:08:11.598
that you can post to
Instagram with a quote

00:08:11.598 --> 00:08:13.460
that would make it interesting
for someone to click

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.399
in your bio to go watch that video.

00:08:15.399 --> 00:08:19.268
I think that it comes down
to the focus of the video.

00:08:19.268 --> 00:08:20.566
What's the take-away need to be?

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:22.778
So that you constantly are
thinking about how to make

00:08:22.778 --> 00:08:24.149
this project better.

00:08:24.149 --> 00:08:26.581
Of little soundbites and
opportunities to leverage

00:08:26.581 --> 00:08:28.744
across all of the social platforms,

00:08:28.744 --> 00:08:29.959
especially in a place like Facebook

00:08:29.959 --> 00:08:31.788
where if you made something for YouTube,

00:08:31.788 --> 00:08:34.434
it's not always the best
project for Facebook.

00:08:34.434 --> 00:08:36.980
So what is really cool in that video

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:37.813
that you could share there?

00:08:37.813 --> 00:08:40.896
I just did, some people know
me from musical book reviews

00:08:40.896 --> 00:08:42.351
that I've done for Gary Vaynerchuk

00:08:42.351 --> 00:08:44.629
cause I guess I'm a Gary fangirl,

00:08:44.629 --> 00:08:47.066
and I finally did my third music video,

00:08:47.066 --> 00:08:48.413
but it was for myself this time.

00:08:48.413 --> 00:08:51.824
And I just did that on
launch day, on the 31st.

00:08:51.824 --> 00:08:54.670
But for Facebook, you
know, it's not quite,

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:57.712
in my opinion, as interesting of a video

00:08:57.712 --> 00:09:00.156
cause you have to really
be moving around a lot

00:09:00.156 --> 00:09:01.618
to pull people in.

00:09:01.618 --> 00:09:04.598
So we just took a 20 second clip of it,

00:09:04.598 --> 00:09:06.262
and uploaded it to
Facebook, so that you could

00:09:06.262 --> 00:09:08.595
click over to YouTube to
watch the whole thing.

00:09:08.595 --> 00:09:10.028
That way if you're pulled in by it,

00:09:10.028 --> 00:09:11.344
you can take that next step

00:09:11.344 --> 00:09:13.262
and have the full experience over there.

00:09:13.262 --> 00:09:15.621
So I think you have to just
think about what is the context

00:09:15.621 --> 00:09:17.753
of every network and how
do we make it perfect

00:09:17.753 --> 00:09:18.859
for that network.

00:09:18.859 --> 00:09:20.445
And that's how you'll find
all these opportunities

00:09:20.445 --> 00:09:23.368
throughout your one video
project to market it.

00:09:23.368 --> 00:09:24.990
- I wonder how much of your marketing

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.886
is based off of leveraging
your other networks,

00:09:28.886 --> 00:09:30.434
like, going to Instagram and Snapchat

00:09:30.434 --> 00:09:33.345
and Facebook and Twitter
and then driving traffic

00:09:33.345 --> 00:09:37.422
back to it versus using
other people's networks

00:09:37.422 --> 00:09:40.125
to, I mean, so like this, you know?

00:09:40.125 --> 00:09:40.958
I'm introducing you

00:09:40.958 --> 00:09:42.091
to my audience.
- Yeah.

00:09:42.091 --> 00:09:43.234
- People may already know you.

00:09:43.234 --> 00:09:45.871
How much of that do you
do with your videos,

00:09:45.871 --> 00:09:47.692
or do you feel like you get the best bang

00:09:47.692 --> 00:09:51.331
just trying to build up your own platforms

00:09:51.331 --> 00:09:52.472
on the other other social networks?

00:09:52.472 --> 00:09:54.636
- I think collaboration
is super important,

00:09:54.636 --> 00:09:57.289
but at the same time, I will
also be the first to say

00:09:57.289 --> 00:09:59.302
I could probably have done
it even more intentionally

00:09:59.302 --> 00:10:01.081
than I have in the last six years.

00:10:01.081 --> 00:10:03.659
But I've actually had some
amazing collaborations

00:10:03.659 --> 00:10:05.338
that have done great work for me.

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:08.067
My personal opinion is if
I continue to make the best

00:10:08.067 --> 00:10:11.163
possible project for my
community as possible,

00:10:11.163 --> 00:10:13.331
they're going to be the best
word of mouth of all time.

00:10:13.331 --> 00:10:14.870
This social proof that Evan's like,

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:16.679
I want to have Amy on and maybe somebody

00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:18.195
will find her interesting
and go over there

00:10:18.195 --> 00:10:21.474
is huge for me, but no
one's going to love me more

00:10:21.474 --> 00:10:24.087
than my community who I'm
making my content for.

00:10:24.087 --> 00:10:26.075
And once you become a part
of it, you kind of grow

00:10:26.075 --> 00:10:27.226
because of that.

00:10:27.226 --> 00:10:29.168
In terms of the social networks,

00:10:29.168 --> 00:10:32.555
I definitely leverage any momentum I can.

00:10:32.555 --> 00:10:34.849
I find my content on
YouTube is so important,

00:10:34.849 --> 00:10:36.294
especially in those first couple of days

00:10:36.294 --> 00:10:38.420
where it's really trying
to find it's footing

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.327
in the YouTube world of
referrals and search.

00:10:41.327 --> 00:10:45.531
I'm going to send everyone to
YouTube to check that stuff out.

00:10:45.531 --> 00:10:48.482
I'm not necessarily going
outside of my own network

00:10:48.482 --> 00:10:49.795
for every single upload.

00:10:49.795 --> 00:10:52.042
I think it has to depend on the situation

00:10:52.042 --> 00:10:53.821
because again, this is for my community.

00:10:53.821 --> 00:10:55.319
They're the best network I could have.

00:10:55.319 --> 00:10:57.058
So I'm going to go to
Instagram and let them know

00:10:57.058 --> 00:10:59.633
with stories that I'm
pushing the publish button.

00:10:59.633 --> 00:11:01.766
And then I go to Twitter
to like drop a GIF in

00:11:01.766 --> 00:11:03.831
to be like, hey, you guys,
there's a video coming.

00:11:03.831 --> 00:11:05.468
And then there's this whole sort of like,

00:11:05.468 --> 00:11:08.512
get the anticipation up, so
that people go and watch.

00:11:08.512 --> 00:11:10.416
I think that's what people
don't think enough about

00:11:10.416 --> 00:11:13.215
is how can you leverage those networks?

00:11:13.215 --> 00:11:14.673
But do it in a way that
makes sense for each

00:11:14.673 --> 00:11:16.267
of those places, so that
people will actually

00:11:16.267 --> 00:11:17.195
take the call to action.

00:11:17.195 --> 00:11:18.696
Cause just because you
posted a link somewhere

00:11:18.696 --> 00:11:20.379
doesn't mean people are going to click it.

00:11:20.379 --> 00:11:22.245
How are you actually making
it worth their while.

00:11:22.245 --> 00:11:24.772
And so that's why I
leverage my own community

00:11:24.772 --> 00:11:26.828
more than I leverage everyone else's.

00:11:26.828 --> 00:11:29.315
But when I actually have a
value prop for everyone else's,

00:11:29.315 --> 00:11:31.122
I'm going to go there and
say, hey, what do you think

00:11:31.122 --> 00:11:33.740
about teaming up on this
because I think your community

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:34.907
would love it.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:37.186
- I like it.

00:11:37.186 --> 00:11:38.019
I like it.

00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:40.158
We're getting a question
from Linger Magazine

00:11:40.158 --> 00:11:42.157
that kind of touches on what
you said at the beginning

00:11:42.157 --> 00:11:44.160
about the different platforms basically,

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.120
trying to understand the difference

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.516
between YouTube, Vimeo, everywhere else

00:11:47.516 --> 00:11:49.302
that you want to be posting video.

00:11:49.302 --> 00:11:50.421
You know, is YouTube dead?

00:11:50.421 --> 00:11:52.128
Should we still be focused here?

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:53.824
What are your thoughts?

00:11:53.824 --> 00:11:55.872
- So I love when people
ask me if YouTube is dead

00:11:55.872 --> 00:11:57.961
because it's the second
largest search engine

00:11:57.961 --> 00:12:00.921
in the world to Google,
and that's its adopted mom.

00:12:00.921 --> 00:12:02.073
So it's like really?

00:12:02.073 --> 00:12:02.906
Is YouTube dead?

00:12:02.906 --> 00:12:05.907
I think what I wish people
would think more about

00:12:05.907 --> 00:12:09.386
is the type of video
in all of these places

00:12:09.386 --> 00:12:11.338
and how different they actually are.

00:12:11.338 --> 00:12:13.647
The other social networks
that are coming to light now

00:12:13.647 --> 00:12:16.958
are actually making YouTube even stronger

00:12:16.958 --> 00:12:20.257
because of the conversation
a video as a whole

00:12:20.257 --> 00:12:21.586
that's happening because of it.

00:12:21.586 --> 00:12:25.599
So let's paint like a really
literal picture though.

00:12:25.599 --> 00:12:27.467
When you go to YouTube to watch a video,

00:12:27.467 --> 00:12:29.224
when you clicked on this video,

00:12:29.224 --> 00:12:33.186
you saw headline and a thumbnail,
and that pulled you in.

00:12:33.186 --> 00:12:35.304
A notification maybe, a
headline, and a thumbnail.

00:12:35.304 --> 00:12:36.388
That pulled you in.

00:12:36.388 --> 00:12:38.081
When you're on Facebook,
you're hanging out

00:12:38.081 --> 00:12:38.914
with your friends.

00:12:38.914 --> 00:12:39.950
You're stalking your ex.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.222
You're checking in on Grandma.

00:12:41.222 --> 00:12:43.265
You know, Grandma's checkin'
in on the grandkids.

00:12:43.265 --> 00:12:44.890
Like, there's a whole
different situation happening

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:48.317
on Facebook when a video
shows up on your feed

00:12:48.317 --> 00:12:51.365
and just starts playing without any audio.

00:12:51.365 --> 00:12:53.697
So that's a very unintentional view.

00:12:53.697 --> 00:12:56.560
So even though we've
been there making friends

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.577
and building communities
so it seems like it's a lot

00:12:59.577 --> 00:13:01.165
easier to get views over there,

00:13:01.165 --> 00:13:02.720
it's a different type of view.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:05.355
We have a little bit of a, not harder,

00:13:05.355 --> 00:13:07.954
but just a different type
of way of pulling people

00:13:07.954 --> 00:13:09.731
in than we do on YouTube.

00:13:09.731 --> 00:13:11.277
So that's why I say a video on YouTube

00:13:11.277 --> 00:13:12.995
is not always the same for Facebook,

00:13:12.995 --> 00:13:14.017
so we have to think about that.

00:13:14.017 --> 00:13:15.744
When you're considering Vimeo,

00:13:15.744 --> 00:13:18.000
usually I say in that case, you know,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.845
is your video a fine piece of art

00:13:19.845 --> 00:13:23.417
that needs to be paid for
or only really respected

00:13:23.417 --> 00:13:24.922
by a very small niche community

00:13:24.922 --> 00:13:28.343
and not on the intention
of being discovered

00:13:28.343 --> 00:13:30.379
on the second largest
search engine in the world?

00:13:30.379 --> 00:13:31.212
Then, go to Vimeo.

00:13:31.212 --> 00:13:32.342
Then that makes sense.

00:13:32.342 --> 00:13:35.012
I will use Vimeo for
more course-like content,

00:13:35.012 --> 00:13:38.068
things that are behind the
paywall or behind an opt-in wall

00:13:38.068 --> 00:13:40.031
or something like that versus YouTube

00:13:40.031 --> 00:13:42.190
just because I like the customization.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.171
But that's just a preference.

00:13:43.171 --> 00:13:44.775
I could use YouTube, too, for that.

00:13:44.775 --> 00:13:46.551
But YouTube, you're being discovered,

00:13:46.551 --> 00:13:48.363
and people are deciding to come to you

00:13:48.363 --> 00:13:50.148
because of your thumbnail and headline.

00:13:50.148 --> 00:13:52.371
It's not the same as an
auto-play on Facebook,

00:13:52.371 --> 00:13:53.432
so there's that.

00:13:53.432 --> 00:13:55.080
Personally, I think video on Instagram

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.271
is what made it almost lose to Snapchat,

00:13:58.271 --> 00:14:00.345
which is why them stealing stories

00:14:00.345 --> 00:14:02.342
made it all kind of work out again.

00:14:02.342 --> 00:14:03.700
Because nobody is sitting on Instagram

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.499
and just watching a video.

00:14:05.499 --> 00:14:07.616
They're scrolling and
double-tapping and scrolling

00:14:07.616 --> 00:14:09.228
and double-tapping and that's it.

00:14:09.228 --> 00:14:10.061
That's all they want to do.

00:14:10.061 --> 00:14:11.685
They want to look at pretty
things and like them.

00:14:11.685 --> 00:14:13.380
So how do you make video for that context?

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:14.847
Now, we see those like Boomerangs

00:14:14.847 --> 00:14:16.810
because you don't have to
pay attention to those.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:17.970
They're hilarious.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:21.060
And then Twitter is
GIF-land in my opinion.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:22.756
Or GIF-land depending on what you prefer.

00:14:22.756 --> 00:14:25.761
You have to remember,
people are on the go.

00:14:25.761 --> 00:14:27.295
What speed are they looking at content

00:14:27.295 --> 00:14:28.357
in all these places?

00:14:28.357 --> 00:14:30.486
How do they like to watch
it, and what makes sense?

00:14:30.486 --> 00:14:33.548
It's going to make you go, oh my gosh.

00:14:33.548 --> 00:14:35.831
Every one of these needs
a different kind of video.

00:14:35.831 --> 00:14:38.314
And that's where the wheels
start to turn on repurposing

00:14:38.314 --> 00:14:40.434
and marketing properly.

00:14:40.434 --> 00:14:41.505
- Love it.

00:14:41.505 --> 00:14:44.064
Sebastian is writing, Evan, how can I buy

00:14:44.064 --> 00:14:45.895
a book from Germany?

00:14:45.895 --> 00:14:47.308
I don't know if he's talking
about my book or yours,

00:14:47.308 --> 00:14:48.347
but mine is on Amazon.

00:14:48.347 --> 00:14:49.180
We have lots

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.055
of different customers.
- Yeah, Amazon.

00:14:50.055 --> 00:14:50.888
Buy 'em both.

00:14:50.888 --> 00:14:51.721
They're on there.

00:14:51.721 --> 00:14:52.554
(laughs)

00:14:52.554 --> 00:14:54.423
- Are you, do you have
like, multiple versions?

00:14:54.423 --> 00:14:55.256
You have an ebook version.

00:14:55.256 --> 00:14:56.429
Do you have an audio version?

00:14:56.429 --> 00:14:57.262
Is that coming?

00:14:57.262 --> 00:14:58.095
What's?

00:14:58.095 --> 00:14:59.524
- The audio version will be out later.

00:14:59.524 --> 00:15:00.884
I have the hardback, paperback,

00:15:00.884 --> 00:15:04.179
and Kindle in Amazon right now.

00:15:04.179 --> 00:15:05.642
So if you go to VlogLikeABoss.com,

00:15:05.642 --> 00:15:07.176
you can find the links
to everything there,

00:15:07.176 --> 00:15:10.165
but yeah, we have
everything except for audio.

00:15:10.165 --> 00:15:11.472
But audio is on its way.

00:15:11.472 --> 00:15:13.102
- Cool, are you doing the audio yourself?

00:15:13.102 --> 00:15:14.355
- Oh, yeah, oh, my gosh.

00:15:14.355 --> 00:15:16.941
So I just did my first like, book reading

00:15:16.941 --> 00:15:19.798
at a signing last night,
which was super emotional.

00:15:19.798 --> 00:15:21.091
So I had a hard time getting through it,

00:15:21.091 --> 00:15:24.071
but I feel like because I make videos

00:15:24.071 --> 00:15:27.325
it's only right that people
get to hear my personality

00:15:27.325 --> 00:15:29.047
come through in my own book.

00:15:29.047 --> 00:15:32.387
There's no way I could
go with any other option.

00:15:32.387 --> 00:15:33.666
- I was the exact same way.

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:34.525
It was fun.

00:15:34.525 --> 00:15:35.358
- [Amy] Yeah.

00:15:35.358 --> 00:15:36.742
- It was pure fire
sitting there recording.

00:15:36.742 --> 00:15:37.575
- [Amy] It's so fun.

00:15:37.575 --> 00:15:40.297
- I'm excited to hear yours.

00:15:40.297 --> 00:15:44.464
Okay, ArtandHomesteading, to
what degree is personality

00:15:46.335 --> 00:15:48.760
more important than content,
more or less important

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:49.709
than content?

00:15:49.709 --> 00:15:52.711
In other words, should
I spend more time on my

00:15:52.711 --> 00:15:56.915
in front of camera skills or
my research slash subjects?

00:15:56.915 --> 00:16:00.320
- I think you need to know your game.

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.647
I mean, that's really
important, especially these days

00:16:02.647 --> 00:16:06.578
where if it's as easy as taking
a selfie to get attention,

00:16:06.578 --> 00:16:09.182
the competition for being a
thought leader in your space

00:16:09.182 --> 00:16:10.678
is going to become much higher.

00:16:10.678 --> 00:16:11.669
You need to know your stuff,

00:16:11.669 --> 00:16:13.265
so content is extremely important.

00:16:13.265 --> 00:16:14.600
I would also say it's important

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.406
because I don't read a prompter.

00:16:17.406 --> 00:16:18.781
I don't have a script.

00:16:18.781 --> 00:16:20.897
So when I'm looking at
the lens of a camera

00:16:20.897 --> 00:16:22.492
like it's a person, I should probably know

00:16:22.492 --> 00:16:24.560
what I'm going to say,
so that has a lot to do

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:27.297
with that, too, just prepping
for that conversation.

00:16:27.297 --> 00:16:28.887
So content is extremely important.

00:16:28.887 --> 00:16:32.185
But that's where the personality
just takes its own way

00:16:32.185 --> 00:16:34.010
into the situation.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:35.839
I'm not customizing my personality, right?

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:37.058
I'm just telling you what I know,

00:16:37.058 --> 00:16:38.209
and so this is my personality.

00:16:38.209 --> 00:16:39.190
It's the one I was born with.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:40.235
It's the only one I get.

00:16:40.235 --> 00:16:41.262
It's the only one I need.

00:16:41.262 --> 00:16:42.979
And that's what, you'll
read that in the book,

00:16:42.979 --> 00:16:44.495
and I think a lot of
people deal with that.

00:16:44.495 --> 00:16:45.439
And I did, too.

00:16:45.439 --> 00:16:48.245
I wasn't a ham for the
camera when I was growing up.

00:16:48.245 --> 00:16:49.939
I just figured out how
important my message was

00:16:49.939 --> 00:16:51.393
and just started delivering it.

00:16:51.393 --> 00:16:52.226
That's it.

00:16:52.226 --> 00:16:53.646
So I think it's knowing the content,

00:16:53.646 --> 00:16:55.119
knowing it really well, and delivering it

00:16:55.119 --> 00:16:56.372
to the person you're thinking of

00:16:56.372 --> 00:16:58.246
and looking at them and saying it.

00:16:58.246 --> 00:17:01.084
Using the camera that way,
and then you've done your job

00:17:01.084 --> 00:17:02.983
in terms of personality.

00:17:02.983 --> 00:17:05.970
Because it's just going to be
you talking to someone then.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:06.803
- I like it.

00:17:06.803 --> 00:17:07.636
Now, you've talked about the importance

00:17:07.636 --> 00:17:09.019
of our message a couple times.

00:17:09.019 --> 00:17:11.676
For people who aren't familiar
with your YouTube channel.

00:17:11.676 --> 00:17:12.509
- [Amy] Sure.

00:17:12.509 --> 00:17:14.371
- What is the message
of Savvy, Sexy, Social

00:17:14.371 --> 00:17:16.172
when they come and check out your videos?

00:17:16.172 --> 00:17:18.331
- So, I mean, I just want
you to come to my channel

00:17:18.331 --> 00:17:21.439
and feel motivated and equipped

00:17:21.439 --> 00:17:23.811
to go after the life you want.

00:17:23.811 --> 00:17:24.644
- [Evan] Nice.

00:17:24.644 --> 00:17:26.764
- And I do a lot of that
through teaching you

00:17:26.764 --> 00:17:29.435
how to use different vehicles
of delivering your message

00:17:29.435 --> 00:17:31.386
like social media and video.

00:17:31.386 --> 00:17:35.630
But sometimes it's productivity
and time management.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:37.067
Because sometimes we just can't figure out

00:17:37.067 --> 00:17:38.695
how to fit everything in in a day.

00:17:38.695 --> 00:17:42.100
And that really keeps
us from getting it done,

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.847
and therefore, we're not
going after the life we want.

00:17:43.847 --> 00:17:45.259
I want everyone to feel like they can.

00:17:45.259 --> 00:17:46.346
It's 2017.

00:17:46.346 --> 00:17:47.713
If you're not happy with your life,

00:17:47.713 --> 00:17:48.852
you can change it.

00:17:48.852 --> 00:17:50.193
And you can make it happen,

00:17:50.193 --> 00:17:53.589
but don't make yourself thing you can't.

00:17:53.589 --> 00:17:56.940
Any video that I have delivers on that,

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.297
and I'm very adamant about
giving you something actionable

00:18:00.297 --> 00:18:03.714
always because hyperbole is wonderful.

00:18:03.714 --> 00:18:06.033
And it's motivational,
but at the same time,

00:18:06.033 --> 00:18:07.757
I need somethin' here.

00:18:07.757 --> 00:18:09.291
Like, give me somethin' to do right now

00:18:09.291 --> 00:18:10.124
cause I'll do it.

00:18:10.124 --> 00:18:12.267
I'll test it, and I will
let you know if it worked.

00:18:12.267 --> 00:18:14.408
And I want my community to do that always.

00:18:14.408 --> 00:18:15.415
So come to my channel.

00:18:15.415 --> 00:18:16.832
If there's something you want to do

00:18:16.832 --> 00:18:18.256
or something you want to say

00:18:18.256 --> 00:18:20.821
or a change you want to make,
and you just need to know

00:18:20.821 --> 00:18:21.904
how to do it.

00:18:23.027 --> 00:18:23.860
- I like it.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:25.067
Well, you have some fans
already in the chat.

00:18:25.067 --> 00:18:28.088
Racquel Carter: I love Amy!

00:18:28.088 --> 00:18:29.568
- [Amy] Thank you.

00:18:29.568 --> 00:18:30.962
- Next question.

00:18:30.962 --> 00:18:32.454
This is a super common question.

00:18:32.454 --> 00:18:34.255
I don't know if you
address this in your book,

00:18:34.255 --> 00:18:36.140
Vlog Like a Boss, but let me know.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:40.307
Kensen, how do I figure out the
best camera and mics to use?

00:18:41.541 --> 00:18:44.251
- I do talk about gear
although I almost thought,

00:18:44.251 --> 00:18:46.144
like, wouldn't it be funny
if I did book about video

00:18:46.144 --> 00:18:47.292
and didn't talk about gear?

00:18:47.292 --> 00:18:48.758
Cause I'm always telling people, like,

00:18:48.758 --> 00:18:50.976
stop worrying about the gear.

00:18:50.976 --> 00:18:51.873
But here's the thing.

00:18:51.873 --> 00:18:55.098
It, there's, it's a good conversation.

00:18:55.098 --> 00:18:56.831
I'm going to start by saying

00:18:56.831 --> 00:18:59.340
do what you can with what
you have where you are.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:00.173
Theodore Roosevelt.

00:19:00.173 --> 00:19:01.630
That's, I mean, like, that's what you need

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:02.463
to know right now.

00:19:02.463 --> 00:19:03.296
I mean, it's 2017.

00:19:03.296 --> 00:19:05.601
You have a way better camera
than I did when I started

00:19:05.601 --> 00:19:07.029
10 years ago, okay. (laughs)

00:19:07.029 --> 00:19:08.762
So just use the thing.

00:19:08.762 --> 00:19:11.596
And that's the basis of
what I'm going to say.

00:19:11.596 --> 00:19:13.706
There are tools to help
you edit on that phone.

00:19:13.706 --> 00:19:17.444
You can edit on web browsers,
and the accessibility

00:19:17.444 --> 00:19:20.021
is unmatched than ever before.

00:19:20.021 --> 00:19:22.043
So there's that.

00:19:22.043 --> 00:19:25.445
Prove to yourself that you need to upgrade

00:19:25.445 --> 00:19:26.644
to get all the fancy gear.

00:19:26.644 --> 00:19:29.155
Like, I make myself feel
the pain of not having gear

00:19:29.155 --> 00:19:30.541
before I'll go buy it, so I know

00:19:30.541 --> 00:19:32.444
I make every dollar worth it.

00:19:32.444 --> 00:19:34.858
But that's when you know
it's time to upgrade.

00:19:34.858 --> 00:19:37.367
And just get the camera
that you know how to use

00:19:37.367 --> 00:19:39.176
and set up your space.

00:19:39.176 --> 00:19:41.503
I love when people just pick
a corner of their office

00:19:41.503 --> 00:19:44.104
or their house and the
camera just sits there

00:19:44.104 --> 00:19:45.233
until they have somethin' to say,

00:19:45.233 --> 00:19:47.116
and then they'll go stand
in front of it and say it.

00:19:47.116 --> 00:19:50.032
Because that's really powerful
when you get into that habit,

00:19:50.032 --> 00:19:51.097
which is what I've always had.

00:19:51.097 --> 00:19:52.111
I've got my bookshelf.

00:19:52.111 --> 00:19:53.613
When it's time to talk, camera up.

00:19:53.613 --> 00:19:54.446
Let's go.

00:19:54.446 --> 00:19:55.279
Lights on.

00:19:55.279 --> 00:19:56.112
Talk.

00:19:56.112 --> 00:19:56.945
And I think that's the best moment

00:19:56.945 --> 00:19:57.778
you can capture with video.

00:19:57.778 --> 00:20:00.042
If that's easier for you
with a smart phone, do it.

00:20:00.042 --> 00:20:01.467
Because you've got it.

00:20:01.467 --> 00:20:02.574
I mean, most of us do.

00:20:02.574 --> 00:20:05.151
And so that's really important,
but prove to yourself

00:20:05.151 --> 00:20:06.397
you need the fancy gear.

00:20:06.397 --> 00:20:08.857
I absolutely have a chapter on that.

00:20:08.857 --> 00:20:10.643
I also have a page on my
website dedicated to that.

00:20:10.643 --> 00:20:13.363
But a lot of people will just
jump on buying that stuff

00:20:13.363 --> 00:20:15.155
and not even know really
what to do with it.

00:20:15.155 --> 00:20:17.800
I would challenge you to take action

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:21.006
on making video before you
worry about the equipment.

00:20:21.006 --> 00:20:21.839
- I love it.

00:20:21.839 --> 00:20:22.672
I agree.

00:20:22.672 --> 00:20:23.505
- [Amy] Good.

00:20:23.505 --> 00:20:24.338
- Mack writing in.

00:20:24.338 --> 00:20:26.576
I'm starting a weight loss journey,

00:20:26.576 --> 00:20:29.121
and I want to start vlogging my journey.

00:20:29.121 --> 00:20:30.393
Do you think I'll have success on YouTube

00:20:30.393 --> 00:20:32.952
showing my journey, and
do you have any tips

00:20:32.952 --> 00:20:34.158
for me to start this?

00:20:34.158 --> 00:20:36.493
- I absolutely think
there's success in that

00:20:36.493 --> 00:20:39.906
because the greatest thing about a vlog

00:20:39.906 --> 00:20:42.744
is you're just simply telling
a story no matter how it is.

00:20:42.744 --> 00:20:44.953
And you are just simply doing that.

00:20:44.953 --> 00:20:47.635
I just met someone the
other day that told their

00:20:47.635 --> 00:20:49.563
transgender transformation story.

00:20:49.563 --> 00:20:52.161
And that was just here's what's happening.

00:20:52.161 --> 00:20:53.315
Just letting you know.

00:20:53.315 --> 00:20:54.649
This is how I feel about it.

00:20:54.649 --> 00:20:55.905
This is what's hard.

00:20:55.905 --> 00:20:56.764
And I'm going to figure it out.

00:20:56.764 --> 00:20:59.475
I feel like anyone that's
going through a process.

00:20:59.475 --> 00:21:01.649
I just documented 31
days of launching a book.

00:21:01.649 --> 00:21:03.857
That's a process, you
know, sharing that story.

00:21:03.857 --> 00:21:07.277
People like to see that because
not only can you tell them

00:21:07.277 --> 00:21:10.411
what you know, but they're
actually seeing the experience

00:21:10.411 --> 00:21:12.632
happen, so they know you know.

00:21:12.632 --> 00:21:16.277
And sometimes people need
that when might not have

00:21:16.277 --> 00:21:19.065
as much confidence in
their thought leadership

00:21:19.065 --> 00:21:20.934
on what they know.

00:21:20.934 --> 00:21:23.962
If they feel like they're simply
telling you what happened,

00:21:23.962 --> 00:21:25.618
then, we're all pretty
clear as the audience.

00:21:25.618 --> 00:21:26.538
Like, well, that's what happened.

00:21:26.538 --> 00:21:28.543
And that's how they feel,
and today's been a hard day.

00:21:28.543 --> 00:21:29.858
Yesterday was a good day.

00:21:29.858 --> 00:21:31.472
And it's nice to see that.

00:21:31.472 --> 00:21:33.519
Weight loss journeys, that's
one of the most motivational

00:21:33.519 --> 00:21:35.988
things I think you could watch on YouTube

00:21:35.988 --> 00:21:38.816
because when you see someone
make a change in their life.

00:21:38.816 --> 00:21:40.765
I think that's why a lot
of us gravitate toward

00:21:40.765 --> 00:21:43.661
this platform and decide to
make video or tell our story

00:21:43.661 --> 00:21:45.585
in some capacity because we've seen

00:21:45.585 --> 00:21:47.239
what changes someone
has made in their life

00:21:47.239 --> 00:21:49.460
simply by getting on video
and talking about it.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:50.793
My tip would be,

00:21:52.591 --> 00:21:53.786
and this is tough for me, too,

00:21:53.786 --> 00:21:55.770
so I'll just put it out there.

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:57.223
Don't be afraid to get vulnerable,

00:21:57.223 --> 00:22:00.228
which automatically what you're
doing is pretty vulnerable.

00:22:00.228 --> 00:22:02.154
But I think you have to be really true

00:22:02.154 --> 00:22:05.544
about what you're
feeling and not every day

00:22:05.544 --> 00:22:07.597
is going to be a great day.

00:22:07.597 --> 00:22:08.866
And you're going to know that.

00:22:08.866 --> 00:22:09.979
It's so hard to look at the camera

00:22:09.979 --> 00:22:12.005
and not be negative, I think.

00:22:12.005 --> 00:22:13.681
I think we all want to be really positive,

00:22:13.681 --> 00:22:15.933
but your, I think that your journey's

00:22:15.933 --> 00:22:17.216
going to be more impactful for someone

00:22:17.216 --> 00:22:19.319
if they really know what
they're signing up for.

00:22:19.319 --> 00:22:20.952
If they're maybe followin'
in your footsteps.

00:22:20.952 --> 00:22:23.500
And to hear you go,
like, I just want a piece

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:25.561
of freaking cake so bad.

00:22:25.561 --> 00:22:26.705
(laughs)

00:22:26.705 --> 00:22:29.756
I think being that transparent
is what gets people hooked,

00:22:29.756 --> 00:22:33.228
and I would say don't be
afraid of that vulnerability.

00:22:33.228 --> 00:22:34.160
- Cool.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:36.173
J Green writing in.

00:22:36.173 --> 00:22:39.741
How do you decide what videos to make?

00:22:39.741 --> 00:22:42.137
- So a lot of it, I have
the fortunate luxury

00:22:42.137 --> 00:22:44.264
of having a community
that tells me. (laughs)

00:22:44.264 --> 00:22:45.864
You know, they will just
tell me all the time

00:22:45.864 --> 00:22:46.697
what they want.

00:22:46.697 --> 00:22:48.806
But what's my promise?

00:22:48.806 --> 00:22:51.873
That's to make sure that
somebody learns something from me

00:22:51.873 --> 00:22:54.595
and has a good time and can take action

00:22:54.595 --> 00:22:55.995
on making a change in their life.

00:22:55.995 --> 00:22:57.152
That's the promise that I've made.

00:22:57.152 --> 00:22:59.555
So any video that I want
to make in that regard

00:22:59.555 --> 00:23:02.004
falls into that category.

00:23:02.004 --> 00:23:03.851
But at the same time, I'm a specialist

00:23:03.851 --> 00:23:05.694
when it comes to online communication,

00:23:05.694 --> 00:23:08.476
so it's going to be toward that area.

00:23:08.476 --> 00:23:09.668
And then you can actually read in my book

00:23:09.668 --> 00:23:11.353
about how I bucket content.

00:23:11.353 --> 00:23:12.869
So what are the different categories

00:23:12.869 --> 00:23:15.041
that I want to play in
and then when I come up

00:23:15.041 --> 00:23:17.646
with a topic idea, it
goes into a category,

00:23:17.646 --> 00:23:19.936
which lands in a place on
the editorial calendar.

00:23:19.936 --> 00:23:22.681
And everything really
like plans itself out.

00:23:22.681 --> 00:23:24.507
Instead of the run and gun, like, oh,

00:23:24.507 --> 00:23:25.340
I'm going to do this video now.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.197
I'm going to do that video now.

00:23:26.197 --> 00:23:27.942
That can start to get a little exhausting

00:23:27.942 --> 00:23:30.328
for people, and you won't
maybe stick with it.

00:23:30.328 --> 00:23:32.185
So I do believe in the editorial calendar

00:23:32.185 --> 00:23:34.374
and setting up for success.

00:23:34.374 --> 00:23:36.413
And those are sort of
the, I plan my buckets.

00:23:36.413 --> 00:23:38.084
And I put content in those buckets.

00:23:38.084 --> 00:23:39.864
It's all about, you know,
bettering your life,

00:23:39.864 --> 00:23:42.129
time management, social media, video,

00:23:42.129 --> 00:23:44.866
and how can I help you do that right now?

00:23:44.866 --> 00:23:46.313
Cause that's what I specialize in.

00:23:46.313 --> 00:23:47.146
Go for it.

00:23:47.146 --> 00:23:49.500
So that's how I come up
with what to talk about,

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.074
and you'll often find
when you do one video,

00:23:52.074 --> 00:23:54.665
what happens in the
comments is they'll say

00:23:54.665 --> 00:23:56.395
another question or they'll say,

00:23:56.395 --> 00:23:58.047
wouldn't it be cool if
she did a video on this?

00:23:58.047 --> 00:23:59.466
And it's just like, write that down.

00:23:59.466 --> 00:24:02.400
Even if you're not ready for
it, or if it's down the road

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.777
or it's not quite a
fit, just write it down.

00:24:04.777 --> 00:24:07.611
Because having that feedback is so huge.

00:24:07.611 --> 00:24:10.652
It is such a blessing that
someone's telling you what to do

00:24:10.652 --> 00:24:12.987
because they're saying we will watch that.

00:24:12.987 --> 00:24:14.088
That's big.

00:24:14.088 --> 00:24:16.439
So really leverage any opportunity to ask.

00:24:16.439 --> 00:24:18.698
I always say when you
don't have a network yet.

00:24:18.698 --> 00:24:20.516
Let's say you're starting with zero.

00:24:20.516 --> 00:24:23.760
Go ask the people you know
what's the first question

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:25.928
you think of, or what's your
frequently asked question

00:24:25.928 --> 00:24:28.070
about what you do really well?

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:31.600
Or see if they even know how
to articulate what you do.

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:34.171
When you ask people
that, they will tell you

00:24:34.171 --> 00:24:35.816
what they're thinking, and usually,

00:24:35.816 --> 00:24:37.452
that's what they're popping into Google

00:24:37.452 --> 00:24:39.289
if that's ever an issue in their life.

00:24:39.289 --> 00:24:41.927
And that's a good place to be
when you're making content.

00:24:41.927 --> 00:24:44.780
What are people Googling
that you know how to answer?

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:46.030
That's content.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.145
- I like it.

00:24:48.145 --> 00:24:52.312
Impractical Jokers Full
Punishments is asking about

00:24:53.679 --> 00:24:57.127
basically, how to use Facebook
to drive more YouTube views.

00:24:57.127 --> 00:24:59.039
Do I upload the video to Facebook?

00:24:59.039 --> 00:25:01.081
Is it just linking it to Facebook

00:25:01.081 --> 00:25:02.909
and it hopefully goes to YouTube?

00:25:02.909 --> 00:25:04.317
Is it doing short clips?

00:25:04.317 --> 00:25:07.628
How do you use Facebook to
drive more YouTube views?

00:25:07.628 --> 00:25:10.351
- So I've done a number of
different things for myself

00:25:10.351 --> 00:25:13.230
for clients, and there's
something powerful

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.512
in being able to upload native video

00:25:15.512 --> 00:25:18.266
because Facebook loves
native video right now.

00:25:18.266 --> 00:25:20.712
So meaning, I'm going to
upload this clip to Facebook,

00:25:20.712 --> 00:25:22.164
not to YouTube, but to Facebook.

00:25:22.164 --> 00:25:24.711
I don't like to upload an
entire video to Facebook

00:25:24.711 --> 00:25:27.819
unless it's been on YouTube
for a certain amount of time

00:25:27.819 --> 00:25:30.190
cause that's where I
choose to put my momentum.

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:31.957
If you would prefer to grow
your Facebook platform,

00:25:31.957 --> 00:25:33.869
then, go hard there and upload.

00:25:33.869 --> 00:25:36.015
But I also would say that
you could do something

00:25:36.015 --> 00:25:38.855
a little different and this
is really powerful right now

00:25:38.855 --> 00:25:42.308
because of how much
Facebook loves live video.

00:25:42.308 --> 00:25:46.475
Going live on Facebook and
saying, or while you're uploading

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:49.371
and saying, you know, answering questions

00:25:49.371 --> 00:25:52.427
and saying, I just
posted a video on YouTube

00:25:52.427 --> 00:25:53.760
and it's about this.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.224
Do you guys have questions about this?

00:25:55.224 --> 00:25:56.406
Or what do you guys think about it?

00:25:56.406 --> 00:25:58.452
Or have you had any experience with this?

00:25:58.452 --> 00:26:01.520
And having a real conversation
with people in Facebook.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:04.146
All the while, your YouTube
link is in the caption

00:26:04.146 --> 00:26:06.703
of that livestream, so that
you get a lot of momentum

00:26:06.703 --> 00:26:09.539
on that post with the
live video on Facebook.

00:26:09.539 --> 00:26:11.594
That's probably going
to be with engagement,

00:26:11.594 --> 00:26:13.990
it could be on the feed for 24 hours

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:15.852
depending on how things go.

00:26:15.852 --> 00:26:17.392
You've got that YouTube
link sitting there,

00:26:17.392 --> 00:26:18.489
and you can get some traffic over.

00:26:18.489 --> 00:26:20.999
I feel that's the best
way to get those links

00:26:20.999 --> 00:26:22.987
clicked over from Facebook right now

00:26:22.987 --> 00:26:25.667
because of how much Facebook likes live.

00:26:25.667 --> 00:26:28.759
But I also love to just post my thumbnail,

00:26:28.759 --> 00:26:30.792
and I will put the
YouTube link in a picture

00:26:30.792 --> 00:26:33.811
of a thumbnail because a
photo is still technically

00:26:33.811 --> 00:26:36.484
a native upload to Facebook.

00:26:36.484 --> 00:26:39.484
So that still works for me sometimes.

00:26:39.484 --> 00:26:41.279
Throughout the month of
documenting the launch

00:26:41.279 --> 00:26:44.839
of the book though, we did
upload the full episodes

00:26:44.839 --> 00:26:49.217
of the videos 24 to 48 hours
after it was on YouTube

00:26:49.217 --> 00:26:51.717
because there was just so
much content to work with,

00:26:51.717 --> 00:26:53.431
and we were moving and shaking a lot.

00:26:53.431 --> 00:26:55.097
So it's the kind of video
that can pull someone in

00:26:55.097 --> 00:26:56.147
on the newsfeed.

00:26:56.147 --> 00:26:57.536
That that helped as well.

00:26:57.536 --> 00:26:59.839
But that wasn't so much
for a YouTube view.

00:26:59.839 --> 00:27:01.321
It was just awareness of the book.

00:27:01.321 --> 00:27:04.107
So think about what the main goals are,

00:27:04.107 --> 00:27:06.629
and then, think about,
okay well, what's the,

00:27:06.629 --> 00:27:08.407
if the main goal is to get
attention on this book,

00:27:08.407 --> 00:27:10.115
then we're just going
to do whatever it takes.

00:27:10.115 --> 00:27:12.174
If it's for the YouTube
view, how do you make sure

00:27:12.174 --> 00:27:14.938
that it gets there efficiently?

00:27:14.938 --> 00:27:16.299
- I like it.

00:27:16.299 --> 00:27:21.123
Hod92, any hints on how to
find the message you want

00:27:21.123 --> 00:27:23.706
to deliver in your videos, Amy?

00:27:25.359 --> 00:27:28.349
- I think you will get
a huge hint if you know

00:27:28.349 --> 00:27:31.743
who you're talking to very specifically.

00:27:31.743 --> 00:27:35.233
And knowing who your
avatar is, what they do,

00:27:35.233 --> 00:27:38.442
what their life is like, and
what keeps them up at night.

00:27:38.442 --> 00:27:40.203
That's going to help shape your message.

00:27:40.203 --> 00:27:42.742
I think you know what you do well.

00:27:42.742 --> 00:27:44.864
And then knowing the person
that you're doing this for

00:27:44.864 --> 00:27:47.386
well is going to help
you figure out exactly

00:27:47.386 --> 00:27:48.969
what your thing is.

00:27:49.803 --> 00:27:52.235
So I'll give you an example of my channel.

00:27:52.235 --> 00:27:54.599
When I first started, I needed a platform

00:27:54.599 --> 00:27:56.800
to become a professional in my industry.

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:59.132
And at the time, the customers
that I was working with

00:27:59.132 --> 00:28:03.022
were small businesses in 2010 and 2011

00:28:03.022 --> 00:28:05.727
that wanted nothing to
do with social media.

00:28:05.727 --> 00:28:07.802
They were told they
needed a Facebook page.

00:28:07.802 --> 00:28:09.855
They certainly weren't
ready for video yet,

00:28:09.855 --> 00:28:12.616
but what do we do with
this Facebook situation?

00:28:12.616 --> 00:28:13.647
They needed to learn that.

00:28:13.647 --> 00:28:15.360
They didn't have a budget for it,

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.086
and they just needed
to do it on their own.

00:28:18.086 --> 00:28:22.051
What I needed to do to show
up and be now considered

00:28:22.051 --> 00:28:24.088
one of the top leaders in the space

00:28:24.088 --> 00:28:26.737
was make it fun for them
because they were doing

00:28:26.737 --> 00:28:28.828
all this research, and it was a nightmare

00:28:28.828 --> 00:28:30.107
and they weren't excited.

00:28:30.107 --> 00:28:31.771
And I just said, I'm going
to teach you how to use

00:28:31.771 --> 00:28:33.534
that Facebook page, but
you're going to have fun

00:28:33.534 --> 00:28:35.496
while you're learning it from me.

00:28:35.496 --> 00:28:37.987
So that was really that, the first thing

00:28:37.987 --> 00:28:39.409
that happened for me, so I knew I knew

00:28:39.409 --> 00:28:40.775
how to manage a Facebook page.

00:28:40.775 --> 00:28:41.786
So I'll teach you how.

00:28:41.786 --> 00:28:43.262
But I also know who you are.

00:28:43.262 --> 00:28:44.289
You're a business owner.

00:28:44.289 --> 00:28:45.728
Maybe you've got a brick and mortar.

00:28:45.728 --> 00:28:47.780
This is a total nightmare
that someone's being told

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.576
there's a new marketing
platform you need to use

00:28:49.576 --> 00:28:51.210
and you need to use it now.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:52.869
So let's have fun in this process.

00:28:52.869 --> 00:28:55.135
I'm going to make my video
short because I know you don't

00:28:55.135 --> 00:28:57.436
have time, and every
single one is going to have

00:28:57.436 --> 00:28:59.576
a tactic for you to take forward.

00:28:59.576 --> 00:29:01.292
All of those things came together

00:29:01.292 --> 00:29:03.805
as the message of the channel.

00:29:03.805 --> 00:29:06.122
And then, you're really getting focused

00:29:06.122 --> 00:29:08.431
around who they are, which
is the most important thing.

00:29:08.431 --> 00:29:09.942
But also, what you're best at.

00:29:09.942 --> 00:29:11.412
I think people start to get in trouble

00:29:11.412 --> 00:29:13.970
when they go, oh, that sounds good,

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:15.733
but they're not the expert on that thing,

00:29:15.733 --> 00:29:17.498
or they're not quite as
experienced on that thing,

00:29:17.498 --> 00:29:19.457
but they still try to make a video on it.

00:29:19.457 --> 00:29:21.286
You want to make sure
someone feels the impact

00:29:21.286 --> 00:29:23.831
and gets the results from
you every single time.

00:29:23.831 --> 00:29:25.414
So stay focused on what you're good at.

00:29:25.414 --> 00:29:29.337
But really understand
where they're coming from.

00:29:29.337 --> 00:29:30.170
- I like it.

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:31.936
Now, tying into the last
question about having fun

00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:33.633
and that being your angle.

00:29:33.633 --> 00:29:35.395
Onassis is writing in.

00:29:35.395 --> 00:29:38.092
Should I present myself as a friendly guy

00:29:38.092 --> 00:29:39.833
or professional guy?

00:29:39.833 --> 00:29:43.104
- I think it should be both
because I think we've learned

00:29:43.104 --> 00:29:45.116
in social media that
people want to do business

00:29:45.116 --> 00:29:46.418
with a person.

00:29:46.418 --> 00:29:48.832
As fluffy as that sounds, right.

00:29:48.832 --> 00:29:52.281
You know, we see fewer logos
these days and more faces,

00:29:52.281 --> 00:29:54.181
and I think that's super important.

00:29:54.181 --> 00:29:56.229
So you should be friendly
because nobody wants

00:29:56.229 --> 00:29:58.091
to work with someone that's not friendly.

00:29:58.091 --> 00:30:00.427
But you can be professional
and friendly as well.

00:30:00.427 --> 00:30:02.156
And you can be professional and friendly

00:30:02.156 --> 00:30:04.859
and stand up for what
you believe in as well.

00:30:04.859 --> 00:30:08.320
And so, I think all of the above.

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:09.987
- I agree 100%.

00:30:11.459 --> 00:30:14.384
Can we focus on two
subjects on our channel,

00:30:14.384 --> 00:30:16.579
and how can we do that?

00:30:16.579 --> 00:30:18.582
- Usually, when this question pops up,

00:30:18.582 --> 00:30:20.600
I feel like it's because someone's like,

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:22.760
do I just come up with a second channel,

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:25.364
or should we kind of exert
energy in two places?

00:30:25.364 --> 00:30:28.534
And I feel these days in
YouTube it's so much easier

00:30:28.534 --> 00:30:30.622
to have different kinds
of content on one channel

00:30:30.622 --> 00:30:32.962
because of the way that it works.

00:30:32.962 --> 00:30:36.116
That being said, the only
reason why two subjects

00:30:36.116 --> 00:30:39.805
would be on one channel is if
they serve the same people.

00:30:39.805 --> 00:30:41.661
So if you want to make those two subjects

00:30:41.661 --> 00:30:43.614
your, sort of, brand, and maybe

00:30:43.614 --> 00:30:45.148
they sound eclectic together.

00:30:45.148 --> 00:30:45.998
That could be the case.

00:30:45.998 --> 00:30:47.833
And you're like, who is
doing something like this?

00:30:47.833 --> 00:30:50.980
Well, if you're the first,
awesome, but make it work

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:53.436
because it's for the one
person that's going to find it.

00:30:53.436 --> 00:30:54.448
And they're going to love it.

00:30:54.448 --> 00:30:57.638
But if you're like, we're going
to talk about dog supplies,

00:30:57.638 --> 00:30:59.798
and we're going to talk
about buying paper.

00:30:59.798 --> 00:31:02.164
If those two things are
not for the same person,

00:31:02.164 --> 00:31:03.892
then maybe they shouldn't
be on the same channel.

00:31:03.892 --> 00:31:05.914
I think that's how you determine that.

00:31:05.914 --> 00:31:06.747
- I like it.

00:31:06.747 --> 00:31:08.503
That's a really good distinction.

00:31:08.503 --> 00:31:12.670
Leroy, how are you able
to get out of your own way

00:31:13.842 --> 00:31:17.104
in taking that first
step in your business?

00:31:17.104 --> 00:31:21.911
- Well, I think I was fortunate
that people were coming

00:31:21.911 --> 00:31:25.563
to me and wanted advice
while I had a full-time job.

00:31:25.563 --> 00:31:28.271
And a lot of people think that it's like

00:31:28.271 --> 00:31:29.613
when you make the jump
into entrepreneurship,

00:31:29.613 --> 00:31:30.664
it's big and scary.

00:31:30.664 --> 00:31:33.651
It certainly is, but I did
a lot of work leading up

00:31:33.651 --> 00:31:35.670
to that big, scary decision
even though it was still

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:38.030
big and scary at that time.

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:40.038
In order to get out of
my own way, I think I had

00:31:40.038 --> 00:31:42.864
to do the work and prove to
myself that I could actually

00:31:42.864 --> 00:31:46.867
execute on what I was selling
and not just say like,

00:31:46.867 --> 00:31:48.336
I'm going to start a business and think

00:31:48.336 --> 00:31:50.300
that everyone was coming my way.

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:51.962
So I actually did a lot of client work

00:31:51.962 --> 00:31:54.557
as what we're calling
these days a side hustle.

00:31:54.557 --> 00:31:56.890
At the time, it was just
like, what I did at night,

00:31:56.890 --> 00:31:58.399
the passion project.

00:31:58.399 --> 00:32:01.043
But I did that for about a year and a half

00:32:01.043 --> 00:32:03.669
before I finally made the
leap to leaving my job.

00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:05.631
And I took a long time
to be like, oh, my god,

00:32:05.631 --> 00:32:06.802
I think I'm going to leave my job.

00:32:06.802 --> 00:32:08.812
This is so crazy, but it still
took me like a whole year

00:32:08.812 --> 00:32:10.776
to get to that point.

00:32:10.776 --> 00:32:12.684
The getting out of my own way happened

00:32:12.684 --> 00:32:16.851
when I just did the work,
and I said, now is the time.

00:32:18.013 --> 00:32:21.322
I have measured my risk, reward, you know.

00:32:21.322 --> 00:32:23.159
I didn't have a mortgage.

00:32:23.159 --> 00:32:24.912
I didn't have any kids.

00:32:24.912 --> 00:32:27.455
And I could kind of make a
big, kind of scary decision

00:32:27.455 --> 00:32:29.323
at the time, and some people
don't have that luxury.

00:32:29.323 --> 00:32:31.103
I think that's what it comes down to.

00:32:31.103 --> 00:32:33.436
I got out of my own way cause
I measured the risk, reward,

00:32:33.436 --> 00:32:35.366
and I did the work, and I met people.

00:32:35.366 --> 00:32:38.555
And as soon as I left that
job, I had full-time work

00:32:38.555 --> 00:32:43.041
coming in as what it was
freelancing really at the time.

00:32:43.041 --> 00:32:44.080
Because I'd met people.

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:46.151
I'd told them I was going out on my own.

00:32:46.151 --> 00:32:47.382
I said, I'm available for hire.

00:32:47.382 --> 00:32:49.256
Do you know anyone that needs this?

00:32:49.256 --> 00:32:52.022
And I got out of my own
way by pitching myself.

00:32:52.022 --> 00:32:54.039
And that's probably
hardest for most people.

00:32:54.039 --> 00:32:57.601
Selling yourself is not
something that we brush up on

00:32:57.601 --> 00:32:59.231
when we think of entrepreneurship.

00:32:59.231 --> 00:33:00.914
We just think we're going to
write a bunch of blog posts

00:33:00.914 --> 00:33:04.088
and make videos and maybe
business will come in.

00:33:04.088 --> 00:33:06.579
You got to sell yourself,
too, and so I think

00:33:06.579 --> 00:33:09.920
that's a big obstacle a
lot of people have to hit.

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:10.753
- I love it.

00:33:10.753 --> 00:33:12.912
Amy, it's crazy how 1/2 an hour flies by

00:33:12.912 --> 00:33:13.856
when we're chatting.

00:33:13.856 --> 00:33:14.689
- [Amy] It flew by.

00:33:14.689 --> 00:33:16.244
- You're getting a ton of love.

00:33:16.244 --> 00:33:17.689
Thanks Evan and Amy.
- Thank you.

00:33:17.689 --> 00:33:18.888
- You've helped me a lot.

00:33:18.888 --> 00:33:19.753
Great questions from everyone.

00:33:19.753 --> 00:33:21.040
Great answers.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.408
I want to put the last one on you.

00:33:23.408 --> 00:33:24.981
I want to talk about Vlog Like a Boss,

00:33:24.981 --> 00:33:26.349
and I want people to check it out.

00:33:26.349 --> 00:33:27.182
- [Amy] Yeah.

00:33:27.182 --> 00:33:28.015
- Got to VlogLikeaBoss.com or Amazon

00:33:28.015 --> 00:33:29.373
or wherever you buy books.

00:33:29.373 --> 00:33:31.600
What's something, what's
an important message

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:34.548
from Vlog Like a Boss
that we haven't touched on

00:33:34.548 --> 00:33:36.755
in this chat that you think is important

00:33:36.755 --> 00:33:38.585
for people who want to
have Youtube success

00:33:38.585 --> 00:33:40.918
that they should know about?

00:33:42.708 --> 00:33:47.286
- I think people try to
figure out how to be creative

00:33:47.286 --> 00:33:48.614
these days in video.

00:33:48.614 --> 00:33:51.237
And like, there's going
to be a clearcut answer.

00:33:51.237 --> 00:33:54.387
But the epitome of
creativity is truly trying

00:33:54.387 --> 00:33:56.437
to find where you are an original.

00:33:56.437 --> 00:33:59.301
And I think I've found a
couple ways that I do that,

00:33:59.301 --> 00:34:02.636
but instead of asking someone
else or asking yourself

00:34:02.636 --> 00:34:03.845
how am I going to stand out?

00:34:03.845 --> 00:34:05.361
How am I going to be creative?

00:34:05.361 --> 00:34:06.446
I think you have to do the work

00:34:06.446 --> 00:34:09.572
because that is going to show up for you.

00:34:09.572 --> 00:34:12.609
You're going to come up
with the craziest ideas,

00:34:12.609 --> 00:34:15.965
and video is the most fun
and exciting ways to kind of

00:34:15.965 --> 00:34:18.932
bring them to life and
have people discover you.

00:34:18.932 --> 00:34:22.531
And that's the scariest
thing about the fact

00:34:22.531 --> 00:34:24.654
that you're doing this is
not only are you so bold

00:34:24.654 --> 00:34:27.634
as to make video, but you're
bold enough to be creative.

00:34:27.634 --> 00:34:29.503
But that creativity is
going to take you places

00:34:29.503 --> 00:34:31.169
that you've never been before.

00:34:31.169 --> 00:34:33.740
So as cut and dry and as black and white

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:35.526
as you might want it to be.

00:34:35.526 --> 00:34:37.148
Here are all the steps,
and I give you a lot

00:34:37.148 --> 00:34:39.058
of freakin' steps in this book.

00:34:39.058 --> 00:34:40.522
You're also just going to find like,

00:34:40.522 --> 00:34:43.543
how did you even come up with that idea?

00:34:43.543 --> 00:34:45.457
It's just going to happen for you,

00:34:45.457 --> 00:34:49.683
and when you feel that moment
of if someone made this

00:34:49.683 --> 00:34:52.628
and it wasn't me, and
it wasn't my company.

00:34:52.628 --> 00:34:54.099
If someone made this,
I would even share it

00:34:54.099 --> 00:34:55.820
with my community.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:57.572
That's how you know you
have something good.

00:34:57.572 --> 00:34:59.875
Something so shareable, you would share it

00:34:59.875 --> 00:35:01.424
with your friends and family.

00:35:01.424 --> 00:35:02.955
That's how you know you're
on to something good.

00:35:02.955 --> 00:35:04.784
And that's how you know you're creative.

00:35:04.784 --> 00:35:07.186
And to go after that
idea and allow yourself

00:35:07.186 --> 00:35:08.070
to stand out.

00:35:08.070 --> 00:35:08.903
We're going to stumble.

00:35:08.903 --> 00:35:11.227
We're going to think things are
cool that aren't always cool.

00:35:11.227 --> 00:35:13.804
But your creativity is
going to come out so hard

00:35:13.804 --> 00:35:15.334
when you use this medium,

00:35:15.334 --> 00:35:18.015
and I hope you'll fully embrace it.

00:35:18.015 --> 00:35:18.848
- I love it.

00:35:18.848 --> 00:35:19.681
Boom.

00:35:19.681 --> 00:35:20.514
Nice, final message.

00:35:20.514 --> 00:35:21.347
